{
 "result": "Built `maze.py` to compute the shortest 4-neighbour path length from `S` to `E` on a grid of `#` and `.` read from stdin (or `-1` if unreachable). It uses a sentinel-padded flat `bytearray` where newline bytes (`\\n` = 10) and `#` walls (`#` = 35) naturally act as row/border sentinels (`< 36`), combined with a smaller-frontier-first bidirectional BFS with loop-unrolled 4-neighbour expansion and in-place byte state marking (`36` for forward-visited, `47` for backward-visited, `46` for `.`). Verified correctness across edge cases and two fixed-seed 2000x2000 mazes (`maze_2000.txt` returning shortest path `4008` and `maze_2000_unsolvable.txt` returning `-1` generated by `gen.py`), achieving a +78% speed improvement over the unidirectional deque baseline.",
 "calls": 15,
 "secs": 688.6
